+++ b/docs/cliffs-data.md
@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@
+# Mapping a cliff for FireSim (cliffs.geojson)
+
+For the person who will map a cliff. It says what the file is, what a photograph can and cannot tell you, the format, and how to add a file.
+The plan behind it is section 15b of `docs/plans/air-slice-wind-detail-honest-speed.md`; the build is lane B of release 0.10.0.
+
+## What this is
+
+Each demo site can have one optional file, `public/demo/<site>/cliffs.geojson`, beside `vegetation.geojson`. In it, someone who has looked at
+photographs of a cliff says three things the terrain data cannot:
+
+- **how much of it is bare rock** (a share from 0 to 1),
+- **what the rock looks like** (a style, from a short list),
+- **what its profile is** (vertical, stepped or undercut).
+
+The app uses them **only to draw the cliff**: how much rock detail the face has, whether trees stand on it, how its ledges, cracks and stains
+look, and how steeply the ground is drawn. **Fire, wind, slope, elevation and every card use the terrain as received** (plan section 4, rule 10;
+the fire model's use of bare rock stays blocked). A test builds Katoomba with and without a file and shows the two scenarios identical, array
+for array, and the same run hash for hash (`src/scenario/cliffs.rule10.test.ts`).
+
+**No cliff has been mapped, so no file is in `public/demo`.** The fixtures under `tests/fixtures/cliffs/` are invented shapes that say so in
+their own words. Do not type a cliff in from memory: a cliff goes in only with a photograph or another source you can name.
+
+## What a photograph can and cannot give
+
+| A photograph can give | It cannot give |
+|---|---|
+| Rock character: how regular the ledges are, whether cracks run up the face, how much is stained, the colour | **Metres.** Depth from one photograph is a guess; a ledge a few metres deep on a face a few hundred metres away is smaller than its error |
+| Bare rock against plants: shrubs on ledges, hanging swamps, vegetated gullies. The aerial photograph and the canopy map look straight down and cannot see these | **Overhangs as shape.** The terrain holds one height per point, so the app draws an overhang as shade under each ledge, not as rock that hangs out |
+| The profile, from a photograph taken side-on: sheer, stepped or leaning out | **Anything the fire numbers use at the metre scale.** The fire grid is 20 to 30 m and the air grid 150 to 200 m |
+| | **A texture pasted on the face.** It looks right only from where it was taken, so the app does not do it |
+
+## The rules the file keeps
+
+1. **It changes the drawing only.** Say so wherever it is shown (the Data sets Terrain card does).
+2. **It says who and when.** The file has a source, a licence, a credit and a day; a cliff may have its own credit and day. The Data sets Terrain
+   card says "Hand-mapped for N cliffs" and names them, and `public/demo/provenance.json` takes the source and licence from the file. The
+   repository is public: use photographs you took, or that carry a licence that lets you publish what you drew from them, and say which.
+3. **It is a person's reading, not a measurement.** Nothing in it is shown as a number.
+4. **A wrong file is refused, not guessed at.** See the end.
+
+## The file
+
+GeoJSON, longitude then latitude in degrees (WGS 84). A height on a position is accepted and ignored. This example is accepted by the app
+(a test reads this block): replace every word of it.
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "FeatureCollection",
+  "source": "Example only: traced by hand from two photographs taken from the lookout, 2026-11-01",
+  "licence": "Example only: the licence of your photographs and of your tracing",
+  "credit": "Example only: your name, as you want to be credited",
+  "capturedOn": "2026-11-08",
+  "features": [
+    {
+      "type": "Feature",
+      "properties": {
+        "name": "Example face",
+        "rockShare": 0.9,
+        "rockStyle": "banded",
+        "profile": "vertical",
+        "note": "Which photographs, and what you saw: ledges every few metres, no shrubs on the face."
+      },
+      "geometry": { "type": "Polygon", "coordinates": [[[150.2993, -33.7358], [150.3006, -33.7358], [150.3006, -33.7351], [150.2993, -33.7351], [150.2993, -33.7358]]] }
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "Feature",
+      "properties": { "name": "Example line", "rockStyle": "jointed", "profile": "undercut", "widthM": 40 },
+      "geometry": { "type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[150.2991, -33.7362], [150.3000, -33.7360], [150.3010, -33.7362]] }
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+**The file** needs `source` (where the mapping came from, in a sentence), `licence`, `credit` and `capturedOn` (the day you mapped it, written
+YYYY-MM-DD), each a few words of text up to 200 characters, and `features`.
+
+**Each cliff** is a Feature with a geometry and these properties. Give at least one of `rockShare`, `rockStyle` and `profile`; a cliff that says
+nothing to draw is refused. A property the file does not know (a typing slip, `rockshare`) is refused too, because ignoring it would hide your work.
+
+| Property | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `rockShare` | The share of the ground that is bare rock, 0 to 1. 1 draws the rock detail in full and keeps trees off. 0 takes the rock detail off ground the slope would call rock (a vegetated face the canopy map cannot see). In between is partly rock: trees thin out from 0.2 and stand nowhere from 0.5 |
+| `rockStyle` | One of the styles below |
+| `profile` | One of the profiles below |
+| `widthM` | Lines only: the width of the band drawn across the line, 20 to 200 m (30 if you leave it out) |
+| `credit`, `capturedOn` | This cliff's own credit and day, if they are not the file's |
+| `name`, `note` | For the next person: a name, and a note of up to 500 characters (which photographs, what you saw). The app shows neither. `id` and `fid` are allowed and ignored |
+
+**Geometry** is a Polygon, MultiPolygon, LineString or MultiLineString. A Point cannot be a cliff.
+
+- A **polygon** covers the 10 m cells whose centres are inside it, with holes taken out. Its rings must close (the last position repeats the
+  first). A polygon narrower than a cell can miss every centre and then draws nothing: the app counts it as "outside" (see the card).
+- A **line** covers a band of `widthM` along it. A line is the quickest way to mark a long face; give it a width that matches the face.
+- **Later cliffs win** where they overlap, each property on its own: a later cliff that sets only a style leaves an earlier cliff's share alone.
+
+**Limits:** up to 500 cliffs, 50,000 positions in all and 1,000,000 characters. This is for a handful of landmark cliffs; mapping every cliff by hand does not
+scale, and the procedural cliffs of the app (plan 9d) draw the rest.
+
+### Styles
+
+A style changes how strongly the face's detail is drawn, relative to the generic sandstone. They are drawing choices, tuned by eye, not measurements
+of any rock. Choose the one your photographs show; if none fits, leave `rockStyle` out.
+
+| `rockStyle` | The photograph shows |
+|---|---|
+| `sandstone` | the look the app draws everywhere: ledges, a few cracks and stains. Naming it says you looked and the generic look fits |
+| `banded` | many regular horizontal ledges and bands, few vertical cracks |
+| `jointed` | long vertical cracks and blocks, few ledges |
+| `stained` | dark and ochre water streaks down most of the face |
+| `smooth` | a smooth, weathered face with few ledges, cracks or streaks |
+| `pale` | pale, bleached or weathered rock |
+| `dark` | dark rock, wet or covered with lichen |
+
+### Profiles
+
+A profile changes how steeply the ground is drawn into a wall, and how the ledges and the shade under them are drawn. Only a photograph taken
+side-on can show it.
+
+| `profile` | The photograph shows | What the drawing does |
+|---|---|---|
+| `vertical` | a sheer face with no ledges worth drawing | sharpens the ground into a wall harder than the generic rule, with fewer ledges |
+| `stepped` | faces broken by wide ledges or benches | the generic sharpening, with stronger ledges |
+| `undercut` | a face that leans out over its foot, with overhangs | sharpens like `vertical`, with stronger ledges and a darker band under each one (the picture's way of showing an overhang) |
+
+## Adding a file
+
+1. Keep the photographs where the licence lets you, and write down for each one where it was taken from, in which direction and on what day
+   (study 01 of `studies/cliff-from-photo/` is the example of what not to repeat: its photograph has no source or licence recorded).
+2. Trace each cliff on a map that shows the aerial photograph (QGIS or geojson.io both write GeoJSON). Keep only the properties above: remove
+   the other columns your program adds, or the file is refused.
+3. Save it as `public/demo/<site>/cliffs.geojson`, then run `npm run provenance`. That lists the file in `public/demo/provenance.json` with its
+   source, licence and credit, and refuses a file that does not state them. The app asks for the file only when the manifest lists it.
+4. Run `npx vitest run src/data/cliffsProvenance.test.ts`: it reads every file in `public/demo` with the app's own checker.
+5. Build the demo site. The Data sets Terrain card should say "Hand-mapped for N cliffs" with your credit; the Bundled demo site card lists the
+   file with its own source and licence. Look at the cliffs in the 3-D view from the front, from above and from the side, on the Low and High
+   picture quality.
+
+## When the file is wrong
+
+The app never crashes on it. A file that cannot be read, or that breaks a rule above, is **not used**: the scenario builds, the cliffs are drawn
+from the terrain alone, and a build warning says why in a plain sentence that names the cliff by its number in the file and its name, for example
+"Cliff 2 (Example line): 'rockStyle' must be one of sandstone, banded, jointed, stained, smooth, pale, dark." The first eight problems are listed.
+A cliff that lies on another part of the site, or is too narrow to reach a cell, is not an error: the card counts it as lying outside this ground.
+
+## What is not built, and why
+
+The photograph-to-depth tooling of the study (`studies/cliff-from-photo/`: depth networks of 1.3 to 1.6 GB run on a desktop) is not part of the
+app and not built here. The models are not reachable from the build machine, and the output is not measured geometry: it cannot feed the fire
+numbers (rule 10) and would need a "guessed shape" label. What this file carries is what a person decided after looking: a few small numbers and
+words, offline by construction like the vegetation and aerial layers.
+
+## Where the code is
+
+| What | Where |
+|---|---|
+| The checker, the projection onto the 10 m grid, the card's counts | `src/scenario/cliffs.ts` (a lazy chunk, loaded only for a site whose manifest lists the file), `src/scenario/cliffsSite.ts` |
+| The styles, profiles and the look code in the rock mask's alpha | `src/render/cliffStyle.ts` |
+| The rock share into the rock mask, the profile into the sharpening, the styles in the shader | `src/render/rockMask.ts`, `cliffSharpen.ts`, `cliffDrawing.ts`, `terrainLayer.ts` |
+| The Terrain card's sentence and the bundled-data card | `src/scenario/datasetRecords.ts` (`handMappedNote`), `datasetRecordsPlaces.ts` |
+| The proof that nothing the simulation reads changes | `src/scenario/cliffs.rule10.test.ts` |
